The bribing behavor was an intentional addition, and is relevant to
the
storyline of corrupted guards.  I'm sure the quest could use more
work,
so if you have any suggestions on what kinds of things to include,
please let me know!

> Also I believe the (intentional?) combination of hunger, luck and
> alignment penalty is unnecessary and makes the earliest game too much
> about luck. I have nothing against difficulty as long as it's...tasteful
> kind of difficulty.

That's pretty much what makes the role difficult other than the lack
of
starting equipment and the iron ball.  The negative alignment I think
is logical in this case.  The hunger and luck penalty are mainly for
the increased challenge - the convict has to survive by his own wits
for a while, and won't be able to pray for help until he has proven
himself.  I think removing these penalties might make him too easy in
the beginning, since he is usually strong, and starts with a "weapon"
that can do 1d25 damage.

That being said, I understand your point about it being largely
luck-based in the beginning: if he doesn't find food or monsters to
eat
early on, then he starves, through no fault of the player.  Do you
have
any suggestions on alternatives?  I kind of like it as it is, but I do
wish there were a way to achieve a similar effect without having it be
as luck-dependant.

